<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finlaggan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Finlaggan is a site on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-hebrides">Inner Hebrides</a>, on two small islands in Loch Finlaggan. It was the centre of the Lordship of the Isles, where the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-donald">Clan Donald</a> were inaugurated and their Council of the Isles met in the 14th and 15th centuries. Excavations have revealed the remains of halls, a chapel and burial grounds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finlaggan.jpg" alt="Finlaggan" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finlaggan_-_Eilean_M%C3%B3r_from_the_north_20120411.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Finlaggan lies on Eilean Mòr in Loch Finlaggan, about 4 km west of the ferry terminal at Port Askaig on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a>. Of the three islands in the loch, two, Eilean Mòr, the Great Isle, and Eilean na Comhairle, the Isle of the Council, together formed the administrative centre of the Lordship of the Isles and the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-donald">Clan Donald</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the first half of the 7th century a monastic community was established on Eilean Mòr, dedicated to or perhaps founded by St Findlugan, an Irish monk and a contemporary of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a>. A building, probably of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and perhaps on the remains of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> fort, was raised there in 1138 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled</a>, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorn">Lorne</a>. The stone castle whose ruins remain dates from the 13th century and served the Lords of the Isles as a residence, stronghold, principal court and meeting place of their clan chiefs.</p><p>Finlaggan remained the centre of the lordship until 1493, when it fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iv-of-scotland">James IV of Scotland</a>, who governed the territory through a tenant-in-chief. Iain Mòr MacDonald, 3rd of Dunnyveg, and his son Iain Cathanach MacDonald were taken prisoner at the castle through the deception of MacIain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardnamurchan">Ardnamurchan</a> and were later tried and hanged on the Burgh Muir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. In 1541 Finlaggan was held from the Crown by Donald MacGilleasbuig, and the castle appears to have been demolished in the 15th or 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Finlaggan Trust maintains the site and runs a museum in a converted cottage, which shows finds from the excavations, from a quilted aketon of sheep's wool worn under armour to a cross linked to the lords. The walls of the medieval chapel of St Findlugan have been stabilised, and several 16th-century graves are displayed under glass. In 2019 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> announced a virtual-reality reconstruction of the settlement as it was in the early 15th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Finlaggan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The lords held council at <b>Finlaggan</b> on Islay, with a council of the isles, and patronised the Iona abbey, galley-building, Gaelic poets and physicians.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-the-isles">Lordship of the Isles</a></li></ul></section>
